commended air speed for tunnel ventilation?</div><div class="opt">A) 0.5 m/s</div><div class="opt">B) 1 m/s</div><div class="opt">C) 2-3 m/s</div><div class="opt">D) 5 m/s</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — 2-3 m/s is effective for cooling birds in tunnel ventilation.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">10. How can you check if ventilation is adequate?</div><div class="opt">A) Smell ammonia</div><div class="opt">B) Check litter moisture</div><div class="opt">C) Both A and B</div><div class="opt">D) Count birds</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — Ammonia smell and wet litter indicate poor ventilation.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">11. What is the best feeder type to reduce waste?</div><div class="opt">A) Trough feeder</div><div class="opt">B) Tube feeder</div><div class="opt">C) Tray feeder</div><div class="opt">D) Automatic chain feeder</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Tube feeders have a lip that prevents feed spillage.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">12. How many bell drinkers for 1000 birds?</div><div class="opt">A) 5</div><div class="opt">B) 10-12</div><div class="opt">C) 20</div><div class="opt">D) 30</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Each bell drinker serves 80-100 birds, so 10-12 for 1000.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">13. At what height should feeders be adjusted?</div><div class="opt">A) At bird back level</div><div class="opt">B) At bird eye level</div><div class="opt">C) On the floor</div><div class="opt">D) Above bird head</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Feeders at back level reduce spillage and allow easy access.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">14. How often should drinkers be cleaned?</div><div class="opt">A) Once a week</div><div class="opt">B) Twice a day</div><div class="opt">C) Once a month</div><div class="opt">D) Never</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Clean drinkers at least twice daily to prevent bacterial growth.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">15. What is the recommended brooding temperature for day-old chicks?</div><div class="opt">A) 25Â°C</div><div class="opt">B) 30Â°C</div><div class="opt">C) 35Â°C</div><div class="opt">D) 40Â°C</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — 35Â°C at chick level is ideal for the first week.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">16. What is a brooder guard used for?</div><div class="opt">A) To keep chicks near heat</div><div class="opt">B) To keep out predators</div><div class="opt">C) To store feed</div><div class="opt">D) To provide light</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — A brooder guard confines chicks near the heat source and prevents drafts.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">17. How much should brooder temperature be reduced each week?</div><div class="opt">A) 1Â°C</div><div class="opt">B) 3Â°C</div><div class="opt">C) 5Â°C</div><div class="opt">D) 10Â°C</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Reduce by 3Â°C weekly until room temperature is reached.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">18. What does it mean if chicks huddle under the heat source?</div><div class="opt">A) Too hot</div><div class="opt">B) Too cold</div><div class="opt">C) Just right</div><div class="opt">D) Sick</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Huddling indicates chicks are cold and need more heat.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">19. What is a common brooding heat source in Africa?</div><div class="opt">A) Electric heater</div><div class="opt">B) Charcoal stove</div><div class="opt">C) Gas brooder</div><div class="opt">D) All of the above</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: D</strong> — All are used, but charcoal stoves are common due to low cost.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">20. What is the purpose of a footbath?</div><div class="opt">A) To clean boots</div><div class="opt">B) To disinfect feet</div><div class="opt">C) To cool birds</div><div class="opt">D) To store water</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Footbaths disinfect footwear to prevent disease entry.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">21. How often should footbath disinfectant be changed?</div><div class="opt">A) Weekly</div><div class="opt">B) Daily or when dirty</div><div class="opt">C) Monthly</div><div class="opt">D) Yearly</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Daily change or when dirty ensures effective disinfection.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">22. What is a key biosecurity measure for visitors?</div><div class="opt">A) Provide them with boots and overalls</div><div class="opt">B) Let them enter freely</div><div class="opt">C) Only allow them on Sundays</div><div class="opt">D) Give them feed</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Protective clothing prevents visitors from carrying pathogens.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">23. Where should dead birds be disposed of?</div><div class="opt">A) In the feed store</div><div class="opt">B) Near the house</div><div class="opt">C) In an incinerator or deep pit</div><div class="opt">D) In the river</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — Proper disposal away from houses prevents disease spread.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">24. What should be done to the house between flocks?</div><div class="opt">A) Nothing</div><div class="opt">B) Clean and disinfect</div><div class="opt">C) Add more litter</div><div class="opt">D) Close all vents</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Cleaning and disinfecting removes pathogens before new chicks arrive.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">25. What is the recommended dilution for bleach as disinfectant?</div><div class="opt">A) 1:10</div><div class="opt">B) 1:100</div><div class="opt">C) 1:1000</div><div class="opt">D) 1:2</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — 1:100 dilution is effective for footbaths and surfaces.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">26. Why is a perimeter fence important?</div><div class="opt">A) To keep out wild animals</div><div class="opt">B) To keep birds in</div><div class="opt">C) To provide shade</div><div class="opt">D) To store equipment</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Fences keep out disease-carrying animals and unauthorized people.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">27. What is the best way to store feed?</div><div class="opt">A) On the floor</div><div class="opt">B) On pallets in a closed room</div><div class="opt">C) Outside</div><div class="opt">D) In the brooder</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Pallets and closed rooms protect feed from moisture and rodents.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">28. What should be provided at the farm entrance for vehicles?</div><div class="opt">A) A car wash</div><div class="opt">B) A dip or spray</div><div class="opt">C) A parking lot</div><div class="opt">D) A fuel station</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Vehicle dips or sprays disinfect tires and undercarriages.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">29. How long should a house rest after disinfection?</div><div class="opt">A) 1 day</div><div class="opt">B) 3-5 days</div><div class="opt">C) 10 days</div><div class="opt">D) 2 weeks</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — 3-5 days allows disinfectant to work and house to dry.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">30. What is a sign of poor biosecurity?</div><div class="opt">A) Clean footbaths</div><div class="opt">B) Visitors without protective clothing</div><div class="opt">C) Closed feed store</div><div class="opt">D) Regular disinfection</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Visitors without protective clothing can introduce diseases.</div></details></div></div></div>

