<h2>Module 3 &middot; Brooding Management</h2>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Pre-Placement Preparation of Broiler House</h3>
<p>Before your chicks arrive, you must prepare the brooder house thoroughly. Start by cleaning out all old litter, dust, and cobwebs. Wash the floor, walls, and ceiling with soap and water, then disinfect with a product like Virocid or formalin. Allow the house to dry completely. Next, spread fresh litter such as wood shavings, rice husks, or chopped straw to a depth of 5-8 cm. Avoid sawdust from treated wood. Level the litter and remove any clumps. For the brooder, set up a hover or brooder guard. A common method in Africa is using a charcoal stove or gas brooder, but electric brooders are also used. Place the brooder at one end of the house, with a guard made of cardboard or metal sheets about 40-50 cm high, creating a circle of 2-3 meters diameter for 250 chicks. Pre-heat the brooder 24 hours before chick arrival to reach a temperature of 32-35Â°C at chick level. Use a thermometer at chick height. Also, provide feed and water. Use clean, shallow chick feeders and drinkers. Fill drinkers with clean water and add glucose or vitamins for the first day. Place feed in feeders 2-3 hours before arrival. Ensure adequate ventilation without drafts. Check that all equipment is working. Finally, keep a record of all steps. This preparation reduces stress and disease, giving your chicks a strong start..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Brooding Temperature and Humidity Control</h3>
<p>Temperature is critical for chick survival. For the first week, maintain 32-35Â°C at chick level. Reduce by 2-3Â°C each week until you reach 20-24Â°C at week 5. Use a thermometer placed at chick height, not on the wall. In Africa, many farmers use charcoal stoves or kerosene brooders. Adjust the stove vents to control heat. If using electric brooders, use a thermostat. Watch the chicks: if they huddle under the brooder, it's too cold; if they spread away, it's too hot; if they are evenly spread, it's just right. Humidity should be 60-70% for the first week. Low humidity causes dehydration and pasty vents. High humidity leads to wet litter and respiratory problems. To increase humidity, place shallow pans of water near the brooder. To decrease, improve ventilation. In dry areas like the Sahel, you may need to hang wet sacks or use a humidifier. In humid areas like the coast, use fans or open vents. Always avoid drafts. Check temperature at least three times a day: early morning, midday, and evening. Adjust as needed. Keep a record. Remember, chicks cannot regulate their own body temperature for the first two weeks, so you are their thermostat. A sudden drop can cause chilling and death. A sudden rise can cause dehydration. Be consistent..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Lighting Programs for Broilers</h3>
<p>Lighting affects feeding, growth, and behavior. For broilers, use a continuous light program for the first 3 days: 23 hours of light and 1 hour of darkness. This helps chicks find feed and water. Use a bright light (30-40 lux) initially, then reduce to 10-20 lux by day 7. After day 7, switch to a program of 18 hours light and 6 hours dark, or 20 hours light and 4 hours dark. The dark period allows rest and reduces stress. In Africa, power outages are common, so have a backup like a solar lamp or generator. If using natural light, supplement with artificial light in the evening and morning. Use energy-saving bulbs or LED lights. Place lights evenly, about 2-2.5 meters above the floor. Avoid bright spots that cause crowding. For the last week before slaughter, you can increase light to 20 hours to boost feed intake. But too much light can cause leg problems and ascites. Always provide a period of darkness. Monitor chicks: if they are not eating, increase light intensity. If they are panting and crowded, reduce. Keep a lighting schedule and stick to it. Sudden changes can stress birds. Record any power failures and adjust. Good lighting leads to better feed conversion and uniformity..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Stocking Density and Space Allocation</h3>
<p>Stocking density is the number of birds per square meter. For broilers, the recommended density is 10-12 birds per square meter in open-sided houses in Africa, but this depends on ventilation. In hot climates, use 8-10 birds per square meter to reduce heat stress. In controlled environment houses, you can go up to 15 birds per square meter, but this requires good ventilation and cooling. Overstocking causes poor growth, disease, and mortality. Understocking wastes space and money. Calculate the floor area: length x width. For example, a 10m x 10m house = 100 square meters. At 10 birds per square meter, you can keep 1000 birds. But also consider feeder and drinker space. Each bird needs 5-7 cm of feeder space and 2-3 cm of drinker space. For 1000 birds, that's 50-70 meters of feeder and 20-30 meters of drinker. Use linear feeders and drinkers, or round ones. Provide enough to avoid competition. Also, provide space for equipment like brooders and feed bags. As birds grow, they need more space. You may need to expand the brooder area or remove partitions. In Africa, many farmers use deep litter system. Ensure litter is dry and loose. If you see overcrowding, reduce numbers or expand space. Plan ahead: order chicks according to your house capacity. Never exceed recommended density. It's better to have fewer birds that grow well than many that die..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Early Chick Care and Placement Techniques</h3>
<p>When chicks arrive, they are tired and thirsty. Handle them gently. First, count them and check for any dead or weak ones. Then, place them in the brooder. Dip each chick's beak into water to teach them to drink. You can add glucose or vitamins to the water for the first 6 hours. Use shallow drinkers with clean water. For the first 2 hours, provide only water, then add feed. Use chick starter feed with 22-24% protein. Place feed in shallow trays or on paper for the first 3 days to encourage eating. Sprinkle some feed on the paper. Make sure the brooder temperature is 32-35Â°C. Observe the chicks: if they are active and spread out, they are comfortable. If they huddle, increase heat. If they pant, reduce heat. Provide a brooder guard to keep them near heat and feed. The guard should be about 40 cm high and placed in a circle. After 3 days, you can start removing the guard gradually. Ensure there is enough light for them to see feed. For the first week, keep them in the brooder area. Then expand space as they grow. Always provide fresh, clean water. Change water at least twice a day. Clean feeders and drinkers daily. Keep records of mortality and feed intake. In Africa, many farmers lose chicks due to poor placement. Take your time and be gentle. A good start leads to a healthy flock..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Monitoring Chick Behavior and Health Indicators</h3>
<p>Daily observation is key to successful brooding. Spend at least 15 minutes in the morning and evening watching your chicks. Look for signs of comfort: chicks should be evenly spread, active, and eating and drinking. If they are huddled under the brooder, they are cold. If they are spread away from the brooder and panting, they are hot. If they are crowded in corners, there may be a draft. If they are chirping loudly and continuously, they are distressed. Check droppings: normal droppings are firm and brownish with a white cap. Watery or bloody droppings may indicate disease. Pasty vent is a sign of dehydration or illness. Check for any lameness, sneezing, or coughing. Monitor feed and water consumption: a sudden drop can indicate a problem. Weigh a sample of chicks weekly to track growth. For broilers, target weights: week 1: 150-200g, week 2: 400-500g, week 3: 800-1000g, week 4: 1.4-1.6kg. If below, check feed quality and temperature. Also, check mortality: remove dead birds immediately and record. A mortality rate above 1% per week is a warning. In Africa, common issues include coccidiosis, Newcastle disease, and heat stress. Vaccinate according to schedule. Keep the house clean and litter dry. If you see abnormal behavior, act quickly. Consult a vet if needed. Good monitoring prevents losses..Trim()</p>
</div>
