?</div><div class="opt">A) They spread away from the brooder</div><div class="opt">B) They huddle under the brooder</div><div class="opt">C) They pant and spread wings</div><div class="opt">D) They are evenly spread</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Huddling under the brooder is a sign that chicks are trying to keep warm.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">5. What is the recommended humidity for the first week of brooding?</div><div class="opt">A) 30-40%</div><div class="opt">B) 40-50%</div><div class="opt">C) 60-70%</div><div class="opt">D) 80-90%</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — 60-70% humidity prevents dehydration and pasty vents.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">6. What is a sign of low humidity in the brooder?</div><div class="opt">A) Wet litter</div><div class="opt">B) Pasty vents</div><div class="opt">C) Respiratory problems</div><div class="opt">D) Chicks panting</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Low humidity causes dehydration and pasty vents in chicks.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">7. What lighting program is recommended for the first 3 days?</div><div class="opt">A) 12 hours light, 12 hours dark</div><div class="opt">B) 18 hours light, 6 hours dark</div><div class="opt">C) 23 hours light, 1 hour dark</div><div class="opt">D) 24 hours light</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — 23 hours light and 1 hour dark helps chicks find feed and water initially.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">8. What is the purpose of a dark period in broiler lighting?</div><div class="opt">A) To save electricity</div><div class="opt">B) To allow rest and reduce stress</div><div class="opt">C) To increase feed intake</div><div class="opt">D) To prevent leg problems</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — A dark period allows birds to rest and reduces stress, improving health.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">9. What is the recommended stocking density for broilers in open-sided houses in Africa?</div><div class="opt">A) 5-7 birds/mÂ²</div><div class="opt">B) 8-10 birds/mÂ²</div><div class="opt">C) 10-12 birds/mÂ²</div><div class="opt">D) 15-20 birds/mÂ²</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — 10-12 birds/mÂ² is typical for open-sided houses, but adjust for heat.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">10. How much feeder space does each broiler need?</div><div class="opt">A) 1-2 cm</div><div class="opt">B) 3-4 cm</div><div class="opt">C) 5-7 cm</div><div class="opt">D) 10-12 cm</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — Each bird needs 5-7 cm of feeder space to eat without competition.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">11. What is a sign of overcrowding in broilers?</div><div class="opt">A) Even spread</div><div class="opt">B) Poor growth and increased mortality</div><div class="opt">C) Increased feed intake</div><div class="opt">D) Better feed conversion</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Overcrowding leads to stress, poor growth, and higher mortality.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">12. What should you do first when chicks arrive?</div><div class="opt">A) Feed them immediately</div><div class="opt">B) Dip their beaks in water</div><div class="opt">C) Weigh them</div><div class="opt">D) Vaccinate them</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Dipping beaks in water teaches chicks to drink and prevents dehydration.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">13. What 