details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — A brooder guard is a low barrier that keeps chicks close to the heat source.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">23. How often should you check temperature in the brooder?</div><div class="opt">A) Once a day</div><div class="opt">B) Twice a day</div><div class="opt">C) Three times a day</div><div class="opt">D) Once a week</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — Check temperature at least three times daily to ensure consistency.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">24. What is the recommended light intensity for chicks in the first week?</div><div class="opt">A) 5 lux</div><div class="opt">B) 10-20 lux</div><div class="opt">C) 30-40 lux</div><div class="opt">D) 50-60 lux</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: C</strong> — 30-40 lux helps chicks find feed and water easily.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">25. What should you do if chicks are panting and spreading away from the brooder?</div><div class="opt">A) Increase heat</div><div class="opt">B) Reduce heat</div><div class="opt">C) Add more litter</div><div class="opt">D) Turn off lights</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Panting and spreading away indicate the brooder is too hot.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">26. What is the benefit of adding glucose to chick water?</div><div class="opt">A) Provides energy</div><div class="opt">B) Kills bacteria</div><div class="opt">C) Increases humidity</div><div class="opt">D) Reduces temperature</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Glucose provides quick energy to tired chicks after transport.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">27. How often should you change chick water?</div><div class="opt">A) Once a day</div><div class="opt">B) Twice a day</div><div class="opt">C) Every 3 days</div><div class="opt">D) Once a week</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Change water at least twice daily to keep it clean and fresh.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">28. What is a sign of a draft in the brooder?</div><div class="opt">A) Chicks evenly spread</div><div class="opt">B) Chicks crowded in corners</div><div class="opt">C) Chicks panting</div><div class="opt">D) Chicks eating well</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — Chicks crowd in corners to avoid drafts, indicating a problem.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">29. What is the recommended floor space per bird at 4 weeks?</div><div class="opt">A) 0.05 mÂ²</div><div class="opt">B) 0.1 mÂ²</div><div class="opt">C) 0.2 mÂ²</div><div class="opt">D) 0.5 mÂ²</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — At 4 weeks, each broiler needs about 0.1 mÂ² (10 birds/mÂ²).</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">30. Why is record-keeping important in brooding?</div><div class="opt">A) To track performance and identify problems</div><div class="opt">B) To impress buyers</div><div class="opt">C) To increase feed costs</div><div class="opt">D) To reduce labor</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Records help monitor growth, feed intake, mortality, and make informed decisions.</div></details></div></div></div>

