<h2>Module 5 &middot; Health Management</h2>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Common Broiler Diseases and Prevention</h3>
<p>Broilers in Africa face several common diseases that can wipe out your flock if you are not careful. The most frequent are Newcastle disease, infectious bursal disease (Gumboro), coccidiosis, chronic respiratory disease (CRD), and fowl typhoid. Newcastle causes coughing, gasping, twisted necks, and sudden death. Gumboro affects young birds, causing watery droppings, depression, and a swollen vent. Coccidiosis is a parasite that damages the gut, leading to bloody droppings and poor growth. CRD causes sneezing, swollen eyes, and rattling sounds. Fowl typhoid leads to yellow diarrhoea and sudden deaths..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Prevention starts with buying chicks from a reputable hatchery that vaccinates against Marek's disease. Keep different age groups separate. Clean and disinfect the brooder house before chicks arrive using a product like Virocid or glutaraldehyde. Provide clean water and feed, and avoid overcrowdingâallow 10 birds per square metre for heavy breeds. Control wild birds and rodents that carry disease. Use footbaths with disinfectant at the entrance of each pen..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Vaccination is key for Newcastle and Gumboro. In many African countries, you can buy vaccines from agrovet shops. Follow the hatchery's vaccination schedule. For coccidiosis, use anticoccidial drugs in feed or water, such as amprolium, or vaccines like Paracox. Rotate drugs to prevent resistance. If you see sick birds, isolate them immediately and consult a vet. Dead birds should be buried deep or burned to prevent spread..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Good nutrition also boosts immunity. Ensure feed has enough vitamins A, D, and E, and minerals like selenium. Stress from heat, cold, or poor ventilation weakens birds. Provide shade, fans, or evaporative cooling in hot climates. Keep litter dry to reduce ammonia and respiratory problems. Record all treatments and vaccinations. By following these steps, you can prevent most diseases and keep your flock healthy..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Vaccination Programs and Administration</h3>
<p>Vaccination is your best defence against killer diseases like Newcastle and Gumboro. In Africa, you can buy vaccines from agrovet shops or government veterinary offices. Common vaccines include Newcastle (Hitchner B1, La Sota), Gumboro (intermediate or intermediate-plus), and fowl pox. Store vaccines in a fridge between 2-8Â°C. Never freeze them. Carry them in a cool box with ice packs to the farm..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>A typical broiler vaccination schedule: Day 1: Marek's (done at hatchery). Day 7: Newcastle + IB via eye drop or drinking water. Day 14: Gumboro via drinking water. Day 21: Newcastle (La Sota) via drinking water. Day 28: Gumboro booster if needed. Adjust based on local disease pressure. Always check expiry dates..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Administering vaccines correctly is critical. For eye drops, reconstitute the vaccine with the provided diluent. Hold the chick gently, put one drop in the eye, and wait for it to blink. For drinking water, withdraw water for 1-2 hours so birds are thirsty. Mix the vaccine with cool, clean water (no chlorine) and add skim milk powder (2g per litre) to protect the virus. Provide enough drinkers so all birds get a dose within 1-2 hours. Do not use metal containers..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>For injections, use a sterile needle and syringe. For example, some vaccines are given under the wing (subcutaneous) or in the breast muscle. Change needles often to avoid lumps. After vaccination, give vitamins and electrolytes to reduce stress. Handle used vials and needles safelyâburn or bury them. Record the vaccine name, batch number, and date. If you see reactions like swelling, it's usually mild and resolves. Never vaccinate sick birds; wait until they recover..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Biosecurity Protocols for Disease Control</h3>
<p>Biosecurity means keeping germs away from your birds. It is the cheapest and most effective way to prevent disease. Start with a perimeter fence around your farm. Limit visitorsâonly essential workers enter. Keep a logbook of all visitors and vehicles. Provide boots and overalls for workers, and a footbath with fresh disinfectant (e.g., 2% caustic soda or quaternary ammonium) at every entrance. Change disinfectant daily..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Clean and disinfect the poultry house between flocks. Remove all litter, scrape and scrub with detergent, then apply disinfectant. Use a pressure washer if possible. Let the house dry for at least 3 days before bringing new chicks. Use a flame gun or insecticide to kill mites and lice. Keep feed in sealed bins to prevent rodents and wild birds. Store feed off the floor on pallets..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Control traffic: all-in, all-out is bestâone age group per site. If you must have multiple ages, work from youngest to oldest. Never borrow equipment from other farms. If you do, disinfect it first. Keep wild birds out with netting. Drain stagnant water to prevent mosquitoes. Quarantine new birds for 2 weeks before mixing. Isolate sick birds immediately..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Personal hygiene: workers should shower and change clothes before entering. Do not keep poultry at home or visit live bird markets. If you visit a market, stay away from your flock for 24 hours. Use dedicated boots and clothing. Dispose of dead birds properlyâburn or bury deep with lime. Compost litter away from the house. Regular rodent control: set traps and bait stations. Keep grass short around houses to remove hiding places. Train workers on these rules and post them visibly. Biosecurity is a daily habit, not a one-time task..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Parasite and Pest Control</h3>
<p>Parasites and pests steal feed, spread disease, and stress your birds. The main external parasites are mites, lice, fleas, and ticks. Internal parasites include coccidia (a protozoan) and worms (roundworms, tapeworms). Flies and beetles can also carry disease. Rodents eat feed and damage buildings..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>For external parasites, inspect birds weeklyâpart the feathers around the vent and under wings. Look for tiny moving specks or scabs. Treat with approved insecticides like permethrin or cypermethrin sprays or dusts. Apply to litter, walls, and cracks. Repeat after 7-10 days to kill newly hatched eggs. For mites, you may need to treat birds individually with a drop of ivermectin or fipronil. Always follow withdrawal periods..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Coccidiosis is controlled with anticoccidials in feed or water. Common drugs: amprolium (e.g., 1g per 2 litres of water for 5 days), toltrazuril (e.g., 7mg per kg body weight for 2 days), or sulphonamides. Rotate drugs to prevent resistance. Vaccines (e.g., Paracox) are available but expensive. Keep litter dryâcoccidia thrive in wet conditions. Use raised feeders and drinkers to reduce spillage..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>For worms, deworm birds at 4-6 weeks if they have access to soil or if you see worms in droppings. Use levamisole or albendazole in water or feed. Repeat after 2 weeks. Flies: use fly traps, baits, and insecticides. Keep manure dry and remove it regularly. For rodents, use traps and anticoagulant baits (e.g., brodifacoum) placed in tamper-resistant boxes away from birds. Never use rat poison in feed areas. Beetles: use diatomaceous earth or insecticide sprays. Always read labels and observe withdrawal periods. Integrate control: keep houses clean, repair cracks, and monitor weekly..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Recognizing Signs of Illness and Mortality Management</h3>
<p>Early detection saves flocks. Learn normal behaviour: active, eating, drinking, and clean vent. Sick birds show signs like huddling, drooping wings, ruffled feathers, closed eyes, and reluctance to move. They may gasp, cough, sneeze, or have a swollen head. Check droppings: normal is greyish-brown with white cap. Abnormal: watery, green, bloody, or yellow. Also watch for sudden increase in deaths..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Do a daily walk-through. Look at birds, listen for abnormal sounds, and check feeders and drinkers. Pick up a few birds and feel their breastâshould be plump. Feel the cropâshould be full after feeding. Check legs for swelling or scales. Examine eyes and nostrils for discharge. Record any abnormalities. If mortality exceeds 0.5% per day, act immediately..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>When you find a sick bird, isolate it in a separate pen. Provide warmth, easy access to water with electrolytes, and soft feed. Call a vet or extension officer. If many birds are sick, collect samples (blood, swabs, droppings) for diagnosis. Do not sell or eat sick birds. For mortality, remove dead birds promptly. Do not leave them in the pen. Wear gloves and wash hands after handling..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Dispose of dead birds by burning in an incinerator or burying deep (at least 1 metre) with lime. Never feed dead birds to other animals or throw them in rivers. Composting is an option: layer carcasses with litter and cover; turn regularly. Keep a mortality record: date, number, age, and signs. This helps identify patterns. If you suspect a notifiable disease like avian influenza, report to authorities immediately. Quarantine the farm and stop all movement. Proper disposal prevents spread and protects neighbours..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Antibiotic Stewardship and Residue Avoidance</h3>
<p>Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria. They do not work on viruses like Newcastle or Gumboro. Using antibiotics unnecessarily leads to resistant bacteria, which are harder to treat. It also leaves residues in meat, which can harm consumers and cause export rejections. As a broiler farmer, you must use antibiotics responsibly..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Only use antibiotics when prescribed by a vet for a confirmed bacterial infection, such as CRD, fowl cholera, or colibacillosis. Never use them as a routine feed additive for growth promotionâmany countries have banned this. Common antibiotics: oxytetracycline, amoxicillin, tylosin, and enrofloxacin. Note that enrofloxacin is banned in some countries for poultry. Always check local regulations..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>When using antibiotics, follow the dosage and duration exactly. Do not underdoseâit fails to kill bacteria and promotes resistance. Do not overdoseâit wastes money and increases residues. Use the correct route: water, feed, or injection. For water medication, calculate the total water consumption for the day and mix the correct amount. Provide fresh medicated water daily. Never mix antibiotics with vaccines or other drugs without vet advice..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Observe withdrawal periods strictly. Withdrawal period is the time after the last dose before slaughter, so drugs leave the body. For example, oxytetracycline has a withdrawal of 5-7 days; amoxicillin 1-2 days; tylosin 5 days. Check the label. If you sell before withdrawal, meat will have residues. Buyers may test and reject your birds. Keep records of all antibiotic use: date, drug, dose, reason, and withdrawal end date..Trim()</p>
<p>.Trim()</p>
<p>Alternatives to antibiotics: improve biosecurity, use probiotics, prebiotics, organic acids, and vaccines. These reduce the need for antibiotics. If you must use antibiotics, work with a vet. Never share antibiotics between farms. Dispose of expired drugs safelyâdo not flush them. By being a good steward, you protect your birds, your customers, and your business..Trim()</p>
</div>
