<h2>Module 6 &middot; Growth and Performance Monitoring</h2>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Recording Daily Feed and Water Intake</h3>
<p>Start by weighing feed at the same time each morning, before the first feeding. Use a hanging scale or a bathroom scale accurate to 50 grams. For a flock of 200 broilers, you might start with 10 kg of starter feed on day 1. Record the weight in a notebook or a simple spreadsheet. Also note any feed left in the troughs from the previous day. Subtract that from what you gave to know actual consumption. Water intake is measured with a 5-litre bucket or a water meter. A simple method: fill a 20-litre jerrycan each morning and note how much is left after 24 hours. For 200 birds, water intake should roughly double feed intake by weight. If birds eat 15 kg of feed, they should drink about 30 litres. A sudden drop in water intake often means disease or heat stress. In Africa, where temperatures can hit 35Â°C, water consumption can spike by 20%. Record both feed and water every day, even Sundays. Use a simple table: date, feed given (kg), feed leftover (kg), actual feed eaten (kg), water given (L), water leftover (L), actual water drunk (L), and any notes like 'very hot' or 'vaccination day'. After one week, you will see patterns. For example, if feed intake drops on a Tuesday, check if you changed the feed brand or if it rained heavily. Keep records for at least three flocks to compare. A cheap exercise book works fine; a smartphone app like Farmbrite or a simple Excel sheet on a laptop is even better. The key is consistency: same time, same method, every day..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Weekly Weight Sampling and Growth Curves</h3>
<p>Weigh a sample of birds every week on the same day, ideally in the morning before feeding. Do not weigh all birds; that stresses them. Instead, randomly select 10% of the flock, but at least 20 birds. For 200 birds, weigh 20. Use a digital kitchen scale with a 5 kg capacity and a tare function. Catch birds gently by the legs or wings, place them in a small box on the scale, and record the weight in grams. For example, at week 1, a broiler should weigh about 180-200 g. At week 2, 400-450 g. At week 3, 800-900 g. At week 4, 1.3-1.5 kg. At week 5, 1.8-2.0 kg. At week 6, 2.2-2.5 kg. Plot these weights on a graph: week number on the X-axis, average weight on the Y-axis. Connect the points to see a growth curve. A healthy flock shows a smooth upward curve. If the curve flattens, something is wrong: maybe feed quality, disease, or overcrowding. In Africa, many farmers use local breeds or improved breeds like Ross 308 or Cobb 500. The target weights above are for these improved breeds. If your birds are behind by more than 10%, adjust management immediately. For example, if week 3 average is only 700 g instead of 850 g, check for coccidiosis or poor ventilation. Keep a notebook with weekly weights for each flock. After a few flocks, you will know your farm's normal curve. Share the graph with your feed supplier or vet if you need help. A simple hand-drawn graph on graph paper is enough. The goal is to spot problems early, not to have perfect charts..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Feed Conversion Ratio Calculation and Interpretation</h3>
<p>Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR) tells you how many kilograms of feed it takes to produce one kilogram of live bird weight. The formula is: FCR = total feed eaten (kg) Ã· total weight gained (kg). For a flock of 200 broilers, suppose you feed 500 kg of feed over 6 weeks. At the end, the total live weight of all birds is 400 kg (average 2 kg per bird). Then FCR = 500 Ã· 400 = 1.25. That means 1.25 kg of feed makes 1 kg of chicken. Good broiler FCR in Africa is between 1.5 and 1.8 for improved breeds. If your FCR is 2.0 or higher, you are wasting money. Common causes: feed spillage, poor quality feed, disease, or too much heat. To calculate accurately, record all feed given, including any that spoils or is eaten by rodents. Weigh feed bags before and after. Also, weigh birds at the start (day-old chick weight, about 40 g) and at the end. For a more precise FCR, subtract the weight of dead birds. For example, if 10 birds died, their weight is not part of the final gain. Use a simple calculator. If FCR is high, check your feeders: are they too full, causing spillage? Are there rats? Is the feed crumbly or dusty? In hot weather, birds eat less and grow slower, raising FCR. You can improve FCR by using high-quality feed, keeping the house cool with fans or open sides, and ensuring birds are healthy. Compare FCR between flocks. If flock 1 had FCR 1.6 and flock 2 has 1.9, investigate what changed. Maybe flock 2 had a disease outbreak. FCR is your best measure of profitability because feed is 70% of costs. A difference of 0.2 in FCR on 200 birds can mean 40 kg of extra feed, costing you real money..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Uniformity Assessment and Flock Grading</h3>
<p>Uniformity means how similar your birds are in size. A uniform flock is easier to manage and more profitable because all birds reach market weight at the same time. To assess uniformity, weigh 50 to 100 birds individually at week 4 or 5. Record each weight. Then calculate the average weight. Next, count how many birds fall within plus or minus 10% of that average. For example, if average weight is 1.5 kg, the range is 1.35 kg to 1.65 kg. If 80 out of 100 birds are in that range, uniformity is 80%. Good uniformity is above 80%. Below 70% means problems. Causes of poor uniformity: overcrowding, uneven feed access, disease, or mixing chicks from different hatches. In Africa, many farmers use round feeders that allow dominant birds to eat more. Switch to linear feeders with enough space: for 200 birds, provide at least 10 meters of feeder space. Also, sort birds by size at week 3. Create three groups: small, medium, large. Give small birds extra feeder space and maybe a vitamin supplement. Grade your flock into grades: Grade A (top 70% weight), Grade B (middle 20%), Grade C (bottom 10%). Sell Grade C birds first as they grow slower. This reduces competition. Use a simple grading box: a wooden frame with gaps that let small birds through but not large ones. Or just weigh and mark with leg bands. Uniformity also affects feed conversion: a uniform flock wastes less feed. Record uniformity each week. If it drops, act fast. For example, if uniformity falls from 85% to 65% between week 3 and 4, check for coccidiosis or heat stress. Uniformity is a simple but powerful tool to catch problems before they cost you money..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Using Data to Adjust Management</h3>
<p>Data is useless unless you act on it. Every week, review your records: feed intake, water intake, weight, FCR, and uniformity. Look for trends. For example, if feed intake drops by 10% for two days in a row, check the birds. Are they panting? That means heat stress. Open more vents or use fans. If water intake doubles but feed intake stays the same, check for diarrhea or kidney problems. If weekly weight gain is below target, increase feed quality or check for worms. If FCR rises, look for feed wastage. Use a simple decision rule: if any parameter is off by more than 10% from your target, investigate immediately. For instance, target water intake for 200 birds at week 3 is about 30 litres per day. If they drink only 20 litres, it's a red flag. Adjust management based on data, not guesswork. For example, if uniformity is 65%, sort birds and give smaller ones more space. If mortality is above 2% per week, call a vet. Keep a log of what you changed and when. After a few weeks, see if the change worked. In Africa, where feed costs are high, using data to fine-tune feeding can save you 10-15% on feed. For example, if you notice that birds waste feed when feeders are more than half full, adjust to fill them only one-third. If you see that birds grow faster when you add a vitamin pack during hot weather, do it every hot season. Share your data with other farmers in your cooperative. Compare notes. What works on your farm may not work on theirs, but you can learn. The key is to record, review, and respond. A simple notebook and a weekly 15-minute meeting with your workers can make a big difference..Trim()</p>
</div>
<div class="lesson"><h3>Benchmarking Against Industry Standards</h3>
<p>Benchmarking means comparing your flock's performance to standard targets. For broilers in Africa, common benchmarks for improved breeds (Ross 308, Cobb 500) are: Week 1 weight 180-200 g, Week 2 400-450 g, Week 3 800-900 g, Week 4 1.3-1.5 kg, Week 5 1.8-2.0 kg, Week 6 2.2-2.5 kg. Feed Conversion Ratio should be 1.5-1.8. Mortality should be under 5% for the whole cycle. Uniformity should be above 80%. Water intake should be about twice feed intake by weight. If your flock is below these benchmarks, find out why. Maybe your feed is low protein. Check the label: starter should have 22-24% protein, grower 20-22%, finisher 18-20%. If your feed is 18% protein throughout, birds will grow slower. Maybe your house is too hot. Broilers need 20-25Â°C. In many parts of Africa, temperatures exceed 30Â°C, so you need cooling. Maybe your stocking density is too high. For 200 birds, you need at least 20 square meters (10 birds per square meter). If you have 200 birds in 10 square meters, they will grow poorly. Use benchmarks to set goals. For example, if your last flock had FCR 1.9, aim for 1.7 next time. If mortality was 10%, aim for 5%. Keep a simple chart on the wall: target vs actual. Update it weekly. Join a farmer group or WhatsApp group where members share benchmarks. The Kenya Poultry Farmers Association or similar groups provide standard charts. Also, your feed supplier often has a performance chart. Ask for it. Remember, benchmarks are guides, not laws. Local conditions matter: a farm in Mombasa with high humidity may have different results than one in Nairobi. But if you are far off, you are losing money. Use benchmarks to identify your weakest area and improve it one step at a time..Trim()</p>
</div>
