ong> — A small batch allows you to learn and adjust before scaling up.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">4. Which tool can help record market data?</div><div class="opt">A) KoboCollect</div><div class="opt">B) Microsoft Excel only</div><div class="opt">C) A calculator</div><div class="opt">D) A notebook only</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — KoboCollect is a free mobile app for data collection.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">5. When does demand for chicken typically spike?</div><div class="opt">A) During holidays like Christmas and Eid</div><div class="opt">B) In the rainy season</div><div class="opt">C) At the beginning of the month</div><div class="opt">D) During school terms</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Holidays often see increased chicken consumption.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">6. What is the main purpose of a business plan?</div><div class="opt">A) To get a loan and track progress</div><div class="opt">B) To show off to friends</div><div class="opt">C) To satisfy government</div><div class="opt">D) To increase feed costs</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — A business plan helps secure funding and guide operations.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">7. In the business plan example, how many kg of feed are needed for 500 birds?</div><div class="opt">A) 500 kg</div><div class="opt">B) 1,000 kg</div><div class="opt">C) 2,000 kg</div><div class="opt">D) 100 kg</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: B</strong> — The lesson states 1,000 kg of feed for 500 birds.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">8. What should be included in a marketing plan?</div><div class="opt">A) How to reach buyers and promote products</div><div class="opt">B) Only the price</div><div class="opt">C) The number of chicks</div><div class="opt">D) The farm location</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — A marketing plan details target customers and promotion methods.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">9. How often should you review your business plan?</div><div class="opt">A) Monthly</div><div class="opt">B) Yearly</div><div class="opt">C) Never</div><div class="opt">D) Only when you get a loan</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Monthly reviews allow you to adjust to changes.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">10. What is a recommended way to handle risks in a business plan?</div><div class="opt">A) Save 10% of profits for emergencies</div><div class="opt">B) Ignore them</div><div class="opt">C) Borrow more money</div><div class="opt">D) Increase production</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — An emergency fund helps cope with unexpected costs.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">11. Which permit is typically needed for a broiler farm?</div><div class="opt">A) Livestock permit from veterinary department</div><div class="opt">B) Driver's license</div><div class="opt">C) Fishing license</div><div class="opt">D) Building permit only</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — A livestock permit ensures health and biosecurity standards.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">12. What is the approximate cost of business registration in many African countries?</div><div class="opt">A) $20-$50</div><div class="opt">B) $200-$500</div><div class="opt">C) $5</div><div class="opt">D) Free</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — The lesson states $20-$50 for sole proprietorship registration.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">13. When might an environmental impact assessment be required?</div><div class="opt">A) For farms with more than 500 birds</div><div class="opt">B) For any farm</div><div class="opt">C) Only for layer farms</div><div class="opt">D) Never</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Larger farms often trigger environmental regulations.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">14. Why is compliance with regulations important?</div><div class="opt">A) It builds trust and opens doors to bigger buyers</div><div class="opt">B) It increases feed costs</div><div class="opt">C) It is optional</div><div class="opt">D) It reduces profit</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Compliance allows access to formal markets and avoids fines.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">15. What should you do with permits annually?</div><div class="opt">A) Renew them</div><div class="opt">B) Throw them away</div><div class="opt">C) Ignore them</div><div class="opt">D) Sell them</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Permits often expire and must be renewed.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">16. How many full-time workers are suggested for a 500-bird farm?</div><div class="opt">A) One plus yourself</div><div class="opt">B) Five</div><div class="opt">C) None</div><div class="opt">D) Ten</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — The lesson recommends one worker plus the owner for 500 birds.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">17. What is a fair monthly wage for a farm worker in many African countries?</div><div class="opt">A) $50-$100</div><div class="opt">B) $500-$1000</div><div class="opt">C) $10</div><div class="opt">D) $200-$300</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — The lesson states $50-$100 per month plus food and housing.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">18. What is a key training topic for staff?</div><div class="opt">A) Biosecurity and record-keeping</div><div class="opt">B) How to drive a car</div><div class="opt">C) Marketing</div><div class="opt">D) Accounting</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Biosecurity and record-keeping are essential for farm success.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">19. How can you motivate staff?</div><div class="opt">A) Offer bonuses for good performance</div><div class="opt">B) Ignore them</div><div class="opt">C) Pay late</div><div class="opt">D) Increase work hours without pay</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Bonuses tied to performance, like low mortality, motivate workers.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">20. What should you do if you hire family?</div><div class="opt">A) Have clear roles and pay them</div><div class="opt">B) Expect free labor</div><div class="opt">C) Give them less pay</div><div class="opt">D) Avoid training them</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Clear roles and fair pay prevent conflicts and ensure accountability.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">21. What is a recommended first step for expansion?</div><div class="opt">A) Increase from 500 to 1000 birds</div><div class="opt">B) Build a processing plant</div><div class="opt">C) Hire 10 workers</div><div class="opt">D) Borrow a large loan</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Gradual expansion reduces risk and builds on existing success.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">22. What is a benefit of vertical integration?</div><div class="opt">A) Reduce feed costs by mixing your own feed</div><div class="opt">B) Increase feed costs</div><div class="opt">C) Reduce profit</div><div class="opt">D) Avoid permits</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Mixing feed can cut costs by about 20%.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">23. What is contract farming?</div><div class="opt">A) A large buyer provides inputs and you provide labor</div><div class="opt">B) You sell directly to consumers</div><div class="opt">C) You farm without a contract</div><div class="opt">D) You only raise indigenous chickens</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Contract farming reduces risk but may lower profit per bird.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">24. How much of profits should be reinvested for growth?</div><div class="opt">A) 30%</div><div class="opt">B) 100%</div><div class="opt">C) 0%</div><div class="opt">D) 50%</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — The lesson suggests reinvesting 30% of profits.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">25. What is a key factor in making your farm attractive to buyers?</div><div class="opt">A) Clean records and loyal customers</div><div class="opt">B) Dirty equipment</div><div class="opt">C) High debt</div><div class="opt">D) No permits</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Good records and a customer base increase farm value.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">26. How much can a well-run 1000-bird farm sell for?</div><div class="opt">A) 2-3 times annual profit</div><div class="opt">B) 10 times annual profit</div><div class="opt">C) 1 times annual profit</div><div class="opt">D) 0.5 times annual profit</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — The lesson states 2-3 times annual profit as a selling price.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">27. What is succession planning?</div><div class="opt">A) Training family to take over the farm</div><div class="opt">B) Selling the farm immediately</div><div class="opt">C) Hiring more workers</div><div class="opt">D) Expanding the farm</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Succession planning ensures the farm continues after you exit.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">28. Why is a written will important?</div><div class="opt">A) To avoid disputes over inheritance</div><div class="opt">B) To increase taxes</div><div class="opt">C) To hide assets</div><div class="opt">D) To avoid permits</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — A will clarifies inheritance and prevents family conflicts.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">29. What is an alternative exit strategy?</div><div class="opt">A) Lease the farm to someone else</div><div class="opt">B) Abandon the farm</div><div class="opt">C) Burn the farm</div><div class="opt">D) Give it away for free</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Leasing provides ongoing income without daily management.</div></details></div><div class="q"><div class="qtext">30. How far in advance should you plan your exit?</div><div class="opt">A) At least 2 years</div><div class="opt">B) 1 month</div><div class="opt">C) 1 week</div><div class="opt">D) No planning needed</div><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="answer"><strong>Correct: A</strong> — Planning ahead ensures a smooth transition and better value.</div></details></div></div></div>

